<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
		<!-- Neither RootNamespace nor PackageId: both default to the project name, which
		     is what they should be. Nothing else ships — everything a generated parser
		     needs is emitted into the consumer's own compilation (docs/syntax.md §6.1) —
		     so a .Generator suffix would separate this package from no other.
		     Folders give the namespaces below it: Grammar/ is the Roslyn-free half,
		     Generation/ is the shell. -->
		<Description>Source generator that compiles .gram grammars into C# parsers.</Description>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageTags>parser;parser-generator;grammar;source-generator;roslyn;analyzer;codegen;dsl;csharp</PackageTags>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>Since 0.2.0. docs/syntax.md is the language, docs/diagnostics.md every message by its identifier; docs/status.md says what this version does and does not do yet.

BREAKING CHANGES, and what to do about each. Regenerate the parser to take any of them.

- The default carrier. GramCarrier.Auto now compiles every grammar it can with the immediate carrier, where it used to only where no construction could run for an abandoned reading. Constructions (=&gt;) then run as their alternative is read, also for alternatives the parse abandons: a factory with side effects, or one that throws, behaves differently, and an exception thrown there escapes the Try methods. In a grammar with a context, constructions run interleaved with guards, switch selectors and external recognizers, so what is accepted and what is built can differ. Accepted parses are typically 30-60% faster and the code smaller; refused input is slower and allocates more. New warning GRAM5016 names a grammar where the tape is the safer choice (a context shared between constructions and guards or recognizers; refusal memory quadratic in the input), and GRAM5012 (information) names the other reasons. To keep the old behaviour, set Carrier = GramCarrier.Tape; naming either carrier silences both. GRAM5015 is no longer reported, and GRAM5007 warns only where Carrier = Immediate was named.
- Ignore-case literals ('x'i, "text"i) mean one thing everywhere: a character matches itself and its simple upper and lower case as Unicode 16.0 gives them, and a case partner on the other side of ASCII is not taken. 's'i no longer accepts U+017F, 'μ'i no longer accepts the micro sign U+00B5, 'σ'i no longer accepts final sigma ς. The generated parser no longer depends on the casing tables of the machine that built it. To accept such a character, write it as an alternative of its own.
- \p{…} reads Unicode 16.0's general categories on every runtime, from a table the generator carries, rather than asking the runtime. On .NET Framework and .NET 8 and 9 a character assigned or recategorised since their tables (U+1C89, a letter in 16.0) is read as 16.0 says; on .NET 10 nothing changes. A parser built in Visual Studio now reads categories as one built with the .NET SDK.
- New warnings, which break a build with warnings as errors until the grammar is changed: GRAM4043, a rule spelled from single characters where trivia is not empty (Std.Digit+ &amp; ('.' &amp; Std.Digit+)? reads "1 . 5" as a number) - join its parts with ~, declare it where trivia = none, or name trivia in it where a space is meant; GRAM4051, trivia not written in braces beside a class that reads the same characters (trivia = ' '* beside Field = [^ ','|'\n']+, whose refusals are exponential) - write trivia = { … }; GRAM4042, a binding power that cannot reach its operand.
- New errors for grammars 0.2.0 compiled and then read wrongly: GRAM3011, GRAM4040 and GRAM4041 for with expressions and calls into a namespace with a rebinding header that were read under the wrong rule (docs/syntax.md section 5.1); GRAM4036, a with inside an instance of a parameterized rule made only under another rebinding, which was dropped; GRAM4038, a recover inside a lookahead; GRAM4044 and GRAM4045, a recover the reading could not carry (inside a rule the lexer reads; a guard or value crossing an inner recover); GRAM4046, a located rule (LocationType) whose value is a Nullable of a struct; GRAM4039, a capture in a step of a left-recursive rule with no =&gt;; GRAM2005, a binding power above 9999; GRAM3022, two imports at one scope that both declare trivia. Each message says what to write instead.
- Rebindings stack: where several reach one call, the one written closer to where the reading starts applies on top of the others and rebinds what they put in place ((X with (D = Y)) with (Y = P) reads P, as X with (D = Y, Y = P)); a caller's with or namespace header applies on top of the header of a namespace it calls into.
- GRAM0010: a project that compiles C# below 8 (the default for netstandard2.0 and .NET Framework) and has a [Gram] is told once to set &lt;LangVersion&gt;8.0&lt;/LangVersion&gt; or later, and no parser is generated. It used to get a wall of compiler errors. The attributes file every referencing project gets is now C# 7.3, so a project with no [Gram] builds at any version.
- Names. GRAM5018 refuses a name that would be written twice into the generated class (a publication called Match or Failure, parse Q as TryParseT beside parse T, two rules that are written alike once their namespaces are joined with _). GRAM1012 refuses a name in a grammar that contains _DotGram. The class's own helper types and pool members now carry _DotGram in their names (Lines_DotGram, Parser_DotGram, …), and a rule named like a member the class writes takes Value after its name. Code that named a generated helper type by its old name follows the new one. Generated helper members with _DotGram in their name are never public: a tagged lexer scanner (ExpressionParser.Lex_DotGram_Tag92 and the like) is no longer emitted as public, and only Scan stays public.
- Binding powers (&lt;&lt; and &gt;&gt;): after an operand read at strength k, the operator loop takes only operators weaker than k. A grouping the strengths exclude, which a when in an operator step used to accept (1+2*3 as (1+2)*3), is refused. A when or lookahead after the right operand no longer hides it from its strength. A grammar read over tokens with no when in an operator step (T-SQL, SQL-92, the expression language) is generated as before.
- Text captures and lookaheads. A text capture of a repetition that may take no turn ('a'{0,2}) is "" where it took none, not null; only ? is an option. A captured lookahead at a rule that builds a value (n: ?=Number with Number : @int) holds that value, as section 3.4 says, not the text it saw.
- Refusal positions. A literal of two or more characters the input ends inside is refused as NoMatch at the first character that does not fit it, and as Starved at the end of the input only where every character there agrees: T = "/*" &amp; ';' refuses "/x" as NoMatch at 1 and "/" as Starved at 1, where both were Starved at 0. The same for a rule read as one scan, over a string as over a reader. A refusal's message now names that place.
- Reader forms. A parse read from a TextReader or a sequence of lines now reads what the same parse reads over a string, and a rule a reader cannot read the same way gets no reader overload, with GRAM5001 saying why: a value collected inside a part that is not a rule call, a part that holds a repetition marked recover (Group = '[' &amp; Row* recover ';' &amp; ']' &amp; eol), a recovery that synchronizes on a recognizer of the host, a choice of literals that can end in two places (("a" | "ab") &amp; "b"), a bounded repetition that can begin what follows it (A? &amp; A+). To keep a reader overload, give the part a rule of its own; otherwise read from a string.
- Trivia is looked up in the namespace chain first, then in its imports, nearest scope first, wherever the using stands. A using at the top of a namespace block used to bring in no trivia.
- The engine's step trace for generator work is compiled where the build defines DOTGRAM_STEPS; it was DOTGRAM_TRACE, which now means nothing.

NEW

- Bindings and back-references: $q: X binds the text X matched, $q matches that text again, X{#q} repeats X by its length - for constructs closed by the text that opened them (Lua long brackets, raw strings, heredocs, Markdown fences). Read over characters, through a reader and a stream; refused over tokens (GRAM4035). docs/syntax.md section 3.5.1; GRAM1011, GRAM2010, GRAM3021, GRAM4032-GRAM4035.
- A trace build: Trace = true on [Gram] or [GramOptions], or the build property DotGramTrace, compiles a parser that reports what it reads to a sink set with Tracing(sink). Three sinks come with it: GramWhy says why the last call was refused, GramTraceLog writes an indented log, GramProfile counts entries, failures and time per rule. A trace build answers what the parser answers; without the option nothing of it is generated. docs/syntax.md section 6.9.
- eos, the end of a segment: a grammar that names it reads a sequence of segments (lines), from a sequence or a TextReader, as the segments are asked for wherever a record fits one segment. GRAM4047-GRAM4050 say what such a grammar refuses. The feeds of docs/feeds.md and the README end their records with eos.
- Std.Nested(open, close), a pair that holds others to any depth: Std.Nested("/*", "*/") reads /* a /* b */ c */ whole, by a counter in braced trivia and in tokens. GRAM4037 refuses arguments a counter cannot tell apart.
- ConstructionsMayThrow = true on [Gram] or [GramOptions]: on the immediate carrier, what a construction throws on a reading the parse gives up is held and thrown only where the tape would have thrown it, so a grammar keeps the immediate carrier's speed and the tape's answers, exceptions included. Off by default; it costs every construction a handler. docs/syntax.md section 7.5.
- PerCall = true on a [GramOptions] that names a LocationType and a Suffix: the grammar is compiled once, and the nested class the suffix names holds the same published methods with locations on. DotGram.Sql's TransactSqlParser.Located is built this way. docs/syntax.md section 6.8.
- A command-line build keeps the parsers it compiled in the compiler server, so the next build after an edit to C# alone does not compile the grammars again; DotGramNoCache turns it off. docs/syntax.md section 6.8.
- DotGramPositionalFollow, an experimental build property for the forms that begin at a position (docs/syntax.md section 6.8).
- New information GRAM5017: a construction of a publication with a reader overload changes the context, and may run a different number of times there than over a string.
- A recover factory is told where reading the bad element stopped and what would have fit there (parserExpected, parserFailureLine, parserFailureColumn, parserFailurePosition), and parserMessage says both.
- DotGram.Cli, a new .NET tool (dotnet tool install DotGram.Cli): dotnet dotgram generate writes the parsers a build would generate, for a project that then compiles them without referencing this package. docs/cli.md.

FASTER

- A run read up to a delimiter, (?!X &amp; any)* &amp; X, searches for where the delimiter may begin rather than trying it at every character.
- A refused input on the immediate carrier no longer builds the standing turns of a loop again on every attempt: a list of a thousand members refused after its last allocated 24 MB and now 96 KB.
- Binding powers over characters: a refused text no longer costs two to the power of its operators (the README calculator refused 1/0+1/0+… of twenty terms in two seconds; five hundred and twelve now take a tenth of a second).
- A grammar of several hundred rules read from a buffer no longer takes minutes to generate.

FIXED (each takes effect once the parser is generated again)

- Generated code that did not compile: a located rule built from its own captures (CS7036); a publication named input or context (CS0149); a reader form of a recovering rule in a grammar with a context (CS7036); an external recognizer or predicate named text, p, c, value and the like (CS0149); a lambda, pattern or query in a when or =&gt; hiding a capture of the same name (CS0103); a recovery in a rule no publication reaches (CS1061, CS0649); a fold with no construction (CS1522); a struct nested in the host shadowing an imported class of its name (CS1503); a recognizer of the host met only in a recovery's synchronization (CS0103, CS0246).
- The generator stopped with an exception on: a left-recursive rule that builds nothing on the immediate carrier ("A value table for 'string' was named"); a guard or a capture written in a recover's synchronization (KeyNotFoundException); wordboundary that can match nothing (now GRAM4001).
- An external recognizer is looked up where the generated call stands, as the compiler binds it, rather than by every method of that name in the compilation (GRAM4015 and code that did not compile). A host recognizer that refuses inside a negative lookahead or an atomic group goes on to the group's next alternative.
- A worded keyword is no longer matched inside a longer word over tokens.
- A rule of a namespace with a rebinding header (namespace N with (…)), called from outside it (R = N.E), is read under the header; it used to be read as if the header had not been written, with no diagnostic.
- A repetition whose turn may match nothing only through an external recognizer no longer loops for ever; the turns up to its fewest are counted however far each moved.
- A guarded repetition with a most ((?!';' &amp; any){0,2}) is held to it over a string; a negative look over a run read from a buffer reads as the string does.
- A reader, a sequence of lines or of segments no longer takes the end of the part it holds for the end of the input where a lookahead calls a rule read as one scan, nor in a choice of literals or a recovery's skip at that end. Before, a reader could accept or refuse otherwise than the string, with no error.
- A repetition marked recover inside another one's element or synchronization recovers as a rule of its own, rather than as a plain repetition.
- A guard or lookahead after the last operand of an alternative no longer hides that operand from the analyses of recursion and strength.
- A located rule (LocationType) whose value is a struct is told its location on the value it returns; with PerCall a mutable struct no longer loses it.
- A @SourceSpan rule with =&gt; returns the span it builds, not its extent. A place past int.MaxValue characters into a streamed input throws InvalidOperationException where it would be told, rather than wrapping round.
- A reader that runs its stack low and carries the reading onto a thread of its own probes the stack again from the first entry when it comes back (ten thousand nested T-SQL CASE ended the process), and reuses one thread for every hand-off.
- TODO(freeze): the reader/engine agreement line - direct and engine readers now agree on outcome, position and value in every form; the details and the commit come with the fix.
- TODO(freeze): the generator crash fix - one line, with the grammar shape it took and the commit.

KNOWN ISSUES

- Over characters, a repetition that gives a turn back reads the turns before it again from the start each time, so a refused text costs the square of its length (a refused sum of 2,048 quotients in the calculator: about half a second). Bound the size of untrusted input.
- By default a parse read from a position or a window can refuse, or cut short, the rule's first reading in a few shapes (an optional or a repetition before a negative lookahead, a quoted string with doubled quotes; docs/syntax.md section 6.8). The whole forms are right. The experimental build property DotGramPositionalFollow fixes them at a cost.
- See the known issues of the release on GitHub, and docs/status.md, for the rest.</PackageReleaseNotes>

		<!-- The symbols inside the assembly rather than beside it. When the generator fails
		     in somebody else's build — GRAM0001, with the exception in the message — the
		     stack trace has line numbers without a symbol server to fetch them from. -->
		<DebugType>embedded</DebugType>

		<!-- Paths in the PDB and the package normalized to the repository rather than the
		     runner's disk, so that what CI packs can be rebuilt byte for byte from the commit
		     its nuspec names. Here and not in Directory.Build.props: it rewrites every source
		     path, [CallerFilePath] included, to /_/, and nine test classes find the
		     repository from their own file — set for everything, it failed eighty of them.
		     Only on CI, because locally it would point a debugger at /_/. -->
		<ContinuousIntegrationBuild Condition="'$(GITHUB_ACTIONS)' == 'true'">true</ContinuousIntegrationBuild>

		<!-- Roslyn component: runs inside the compiler, ships as an analyzer. -->
		<IsRoslynComponent>true</IsRoslynComponent>
		<EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>true</EnforceExtendedAnalyzerRules>
		<IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
		<DevelopmentDependency>true</DevelopmentDependency>
		<!-- Expected for an analyzer package: it carries no lib/ assemblies by design. -->
		<NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128</NoWarn>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Optimized whatever the consumer's configuration is. An analyzer is built in the
	     configuration of the project that references it, and a Debug build of the
	     generator normalizes the SQL grammars with no JIT optimization and every
	     Debug.Assert live: the SQL parsers rebuilt in 56 s that way, 20 s this way, 16 s
	     in Release. The consumer's configuration changes nothing the generator emits,
	     and it should change nothing about how long it takes either. Stepping through
	     the generator itself is the one reason to want it otherwise, and that is what
	     `-p:DebugGenerator=true` is for. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug' and '$(DebugGenerator)' != 'true'">
		<Optimize>true</Optimize>
		<DefineConstants>$(DefineConstants.Replace('DEBUG', ''))</DefineConstants>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<PropertyGroup Label="Enabled Polyfills">
		<!-- netstandard2.0 is forced on us by Roslyn; these bring back the modern
		     types without adding a runtime dependency — Meziantou.Polyfill is itself
		     a generator and emits them as internal.
		     https://github.com/meziantou/Meziantou.Polyfill -->
		<Polyfill></Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.HashCode</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Index</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Range</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Diagnostics.CodeAnalysis</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CallerArgumentExpressionAttribute</Polyfill>
		<!-- `required` needs both of these on netstandard2.0. -->
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.CompilerFeatureRequiredAttribute</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.IsExternalInit</Polyfill>
		<Polyfill>$(Polyfill)|T:System.Runtime.CompilerServices.RequiredMemberAttribute</Polyfill>
		<MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>$(Polyfill)</MeziantouPolyfill_IncludedPolyfills>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- Pinned to the oldest Roslyn we intend to load into: a generator built
		     against an older Roslyn runs on newer hosts, but not the other way round. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.CSharp" PrivateAssets="all" />
		<PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.Analyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
		<PackageReference Include="Meziantou.Polyfill" PrivateAssets="all" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<!-- The analyzer SDK picks these up as AdditionalFiles on its own; they only
		     need taking out of the default None glob so they appear once. -->
		<None Remove="AnalyzerReleases.*.md" />

		<!-- The standard library (docs/syntax.md §5.2) travels inside the generator: a
		     grammar that writes `using Std;` gets it spliced on, and there is no other
		     file for it to come from. -->
		<EmbeddedResource Include="Grammar\Std.gram" LogicalName="DotGram.Std.gram" />

		<!-- The writers of the case-fold and category tables are file-based apps (top-level statements, run with
		     `dotnet run`), not part of the generator: the default globbing would compile them in. -->
		<Compile Remove="Grammar/Model/CaseFold.generate.cs" />
		<Compile Remove="Grammar/UnicodeCategories.generate.cs" />

		<!-- The package's own README, not the repository's. The two answer different
		     questions: the front page argues for the project and the package tells
		     somebody who has already installed it what to write. It links back rather
		     than repeating what it links to. -->
		<None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="build/DotGram.targets" Pack="true" PackagePath="build/" />

		<!-- And the skill beside it, so that an agent working in a project that has
		     restored this package can read how to write a grammar without leaving the
		     machine. The README says where it is, in a comment nothing renders. -->
		<None Include="SKILL.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- Puts the assembly in the analyzer folder of the package. Done as a pack-time
	     target rather than a None item pointing into bin: a None item makes Solution
	     Explorer materialise a bin node even with Visible="false". -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>$(TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage);PackGeneratorAsAnalyzer</TargetsForTfmSpecificContentInPackage>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<Target Name="PackGeneratorAsAnalyzer">
		<ItemGroup>
			<TfmSpecificPackageFile Include="$(TargetPath)" PackagePath="analyzers/dotnet/cs" />
		</ItemGroup>
	</Target>

</Project>
